Vikings were some of the fiercest  fighters in human history using their  impressive battle prowess to spread  Norse influence between the 8th and 11th  centuries their bravery as Warriors was  driven by one ultimate desire to join  Odin in Valhalla only the most worthy  Vikings would get to spend their  afterlives in that hallowed Hall and the  choice of who made the cut fell to a  group of powerful but mysterious women  I'm talking about the Valkyries  oh  most of what we know about the Valkyries  comes from the prose and poetic ettas  collections of Mythology and heroic  Tales written down in the 13th century  in the text these powerful women were  often described as beautiful maidens  decked out in battle gear and armed to  the teeth with brutal weapons using  their powers of foresight and mystical  abilities to sway the tides of War the  Valkyries were said to fly over  battlefields on horses or sometimes they  would sport their own wings like the  goddess Freya who may have been the  first Valkyrie the Valkyries were at  least as deadly as they were beautiful  and were said to be blessed with  superhuman strength and speed in some  stories they could even shapeshift into  different birds most Valkyries went  unnamed but those who weren't were given  War themed monikers like skogle sigrun  or lock which means Shaker Victory Rune  and clashing battle  the word Valkyrie means Chooser of the  slain and they were indeed as selective  as the name implies only half of the  Norse Warriors who died in battle were  carried by the Valkyries to Valhalla the  rest were Spirited Away to falkwanger to  spend their afterlives with Freya once  in Valhalla the dead heroes are on  heriar as they were called would spend  their days fighting in Knights feasting  preparing to defend ozguard in the  world's last battle  despite the power they wielded on the  battlefield the valkyrie's role was  relegated to that of servant in Odin's  Hall the me that the anheriar drank to  heal their wounds from training was  served by the very same hands that  plucked them away from midgard in  addition to deciding individual Warriors  Fates the Valkyries were sometimes said  to determine the outcome of entire  battles an Old Norse poem called  hakunarm recounts how two Valkyries  named skogel and gondal influenced the  Battle of fitchar fought in Norway in  961 CE King Hawk on the good faced an  army led by his nephews who wanted to  take his throne the poet who  immortalized The Clash wrote that hawkon  tore off his male suit and with his  sword hacked through his opponent's  armor as if it were cutting through  water the woodmeuse Surfers were  defeated but King hakun suffered moral  injuries and when the Valkyries appeared  to take him away he asked skogel and  gondal why they came for him when he  fought so well and defeated his nephews  the Valkyries replied that it was only  because they were with him in battle  that he was able to hold the field and  his enemies fled way to kick a man while  he's down in their capacity to predict  and sway the outcome of war the  Valkyries were similar to norns deities  of Destiny who you may remember from our  episode on the Greek faiths a poem found  in Niel Saga from the 13th century makes  this comparison abundantly clear when  the titular darud happened upon a group  of Valkyries weaving the fate of a  battle later that day darood spied 12  Valkyries working around a gruesome Loom  with human heads for weights and swords  for shuttles the thread was made from  Human entrails and the Valkyries sang  while they wove their war-winning wolf  the Nords weren't the only mythical  figures who blended with the Valkyries  by the time the atas were written the  Valkyries had taken on characteristics  of the German Swan Maiden like selkis  from Celtic folklore Swan maidens were  able to shift from their animal form  into beautiful women their good looks  were often their downfall however as was  common in stories for men to steal a  maiden Swan suit and without her  feathers she was stuck in human form  even Braun Hilder one of the most famous  Valkyries fell prey to this avian theft  while the man involved suffered very  little consequence of course brunhilder  went on to live a life of punishment for  his foul deed according to the volsung  Saga brunhilder was the human daughter  of King budley lovely enough that Odin  picked her to be one of his Valkyries  one day brunoilder and her sisters were  out bathing in a pool with their Swan  suits tucked away on the bank to stay  dry unbeknownst to the young Valkyries a  prince named agnar was hiding waiting  for them to be distracted so he could  steal the maiden's feather suits  brnhilder and her sisters were stuck  with agnar for years and whether through  coercion real love or some Twisted  combination of the two Brynn Hilder  ended up making an oath of fealty to  agnar later after he returned  bernhilder's feathers agnar fought  against jagner king of the Goths who was  favored by Odin to win the battle but  when Hilder used her Valkyrie powers to  uphold her both and sway the outcome  agnar emerged a Victorious Underdog and  Odin was Furious his punishment for Bryn  hilder's betrayal was to banish her to a  fully mortal life slumbering in a castle  guarded by a wall of fire an early  Sleeping Beauty if you will maybe you  can guess what happens next a hero named  sigard AKA Siegfried the dragon slayer  was brave enough to pass through the  flames and immediately fell in love with  Bruno but left her behind while he ran  an errand in burgundy while there he met  a jealous sorceress who wanted him to  marry her daughter so she cast a spell  on him and sent her son Gunner to Mary  brunhilder instead except gutter was too  much of a coward to walk through her  protective fire and sigured how to do it  disguised as Gunner renhilder married  Gunner based on that lie and when she  found out the truth she arranged for  Sigrid to be murdered and then threw  herself onto his funeral pyre I swear  modern-day soap opera should dip into  Norse folklore for inspiration Brent  Hilder is fascinating albeit tragic life  illustrates the Mysteries surrounding  the mythical Valkyrie she was a human  woman trained to be a shield Maiden  granted Divinity by Odin who made  herself vulnerable by shifting into a  swan scholars believe that the basic  idea of a Scandinavian War goddess is  quite old but that these varying  characteristics were added to the  mythology over time they all would have  been written down together when the atas  were composed in the 13th century but  that begs the question where did the  Valkyries come from one hypothesis is  that the legend arose from the totally  mundane phenomenon of the Norse Shield  Maiden these were warrior women who  fought alongside male Vikings in battle  modern scientists have used DNA evidence  to confirm that Norse women did indeed  fight with men and it's not too  difficult to see how this practice might  lead to myths about ferocious women who  decided the fate of War it would also  explain why so many Valkyries are said  to have human parents another idea is  that the stories were inspired by  priestesses of Odin and the early Viking  age these women would have overseen  sacrificial rituals in Odin's honor  literally choosing who would be slain in  his name this sounds familiar and it  would explain why Valkyries were  described as the physical manifestations  of Odin's will in the older mythological  poems of the ettas we may never know the  valkyrie's true Origins but we can say  for certain that they have made a large  impact on our popular culture Valkyries  can be found in comics anime television  and video games in the 1850s Richard  Wagner composed his Opera di valkara or  the Valkyries which was one of his four  music dramas inspired by Norse mythology  this one Loosely follows brunhilder's  story and features the absurdly catchy  tune The Ride of the Valkyries and of  course we can see Valkyrie on the Silver  Screen played by Tessa Thompson and  Marvel's Thor franchise the valkyrie is  depicted as a strong powerful woman with  influence over life and death even today  it may seem strange that a culture so  Infamous for its physical dominance  would hold women in such high esteem but  women had more rights in Scandinavian  culture than you might think their  gender roles were typical with men doing  most of the physical labor and women  taking care of the home but women could  own property divorce their husbands and  often had a say in who their parents  marry them off to naturally people with  so many Fierce female figures in their  mythology would have respect for Mortal  women we clearly haven't forgotten the  valkyrie so let's not forget this lesson  they teach us  [Music]
 

